sion and run all cells. If Colab asks for the CSV again, upload the same file. Fix errors and save. Run the final submission cell.\\n\", \"5. Click **Submit test** at the bottom of the notebook. Authorize your own Google account if asked. The button saves a snapshot and prepares a form link. Open that link using the same account, check your details, tick the email confirmation box and press **Submit** on the form. Keep the confirmation. The notebook button alone is not a completed submission. No manual download or file upload is needed. Keep the snapshot in Drive until grades are released.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Timing:** 15 minutes setup, 120 minutes coding, 25 minutes interpretation and checking, 20 minutes final save and submission (180 minutes total). The invigilator announces the exact start and deadline. Report connection problems immediately.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Rules:** Colab and the submission form are permitted. No AI-generated solutions, messaging, collaboration or copied answers. Follow the invigilator's rules for other resources. The lecturer's isolated grader reruns your code on unseen data; stored outputs and claimed marks are not trusted. Partial credit is available for independent checked outputs. An error in one task does not automatically zero the entire test.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Do not submit:** ZIP, CSV, PDF, screenshots or grading scripts. No separate written report. A replacement before the deadline must contain the complete notebook. The latest on-time submission is reviewed for marking; earlier attempts remain in the audit log. If that attempt has a technical or identity problem, its mark stays pending until resolved.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Submission:** use the generated link from the notebook’s Submit test button. A general form link will not contain your prepared snapshot.\\n\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"FULL_NAME = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"EMAIL = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"STUDENT_ID = \\\"\\\"  # Your Student Registration Number exactly as on your university record\\n\", \"COHORT = \\\"CML3112\\\"\\n\", \"TEST_ID = \\\"TEST2\\\"\\n\", \"VARIANT = \\\"A\\\"\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Understand the data\\n\", \"Each row is one synthetic observation of building cooling load. `record_id` identifies a record; it is not time. `equipment_id` identifies equipment and repeats across observations. `load_pct` is load in percent (valid range 0–100 inclusive). `ambient_c` is ambient temperature in degrees Celsius. `target` is building cooling load, measured in **kW**. Blank CSV values become missing values. These data are for teaching, not operational decisions.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A DataFrame is a table. The supplied helper converts it to a list of dictionaries: one dictionary per row, column names as keys, with missing values represented by `None`. Your functions accept these lists and return the stated structures. Keep function names, argument order and dictionary keys exactly as printed. Keep full numerical precision.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"import pandas as pd\\n\", \"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\", \"\\n\", \"def records_from_table(table):\\n\", \"    objects = table.astype(object)\\n\", \"    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict(\\\"records\\\")\\n\", \"\\n\", \"raw_table = pd.read_csv(\\\"engineering_data.csv\\\")\\n\", \"records = records_from_table(raw_table)\\n\", \"print(raw_table.head())\\n\", \"print(\\\"Rows:\\\", len(raw_table))\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Supplied preparation — not graded\\n\", \"Keep the first copy of each record ID, exclude rows without a target and rows outside the valid load range. These are fixed row rules, not learned model parameters. **Do not fill missing ambient temperatures here.** Your model function must learn its median only from training records using `SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\")`, followed by the model in a scikit-learn pipeline. Features are `load_pct` then `ambient_c`, in that order. The target must not be a feature. Tasks 4 to 8 receive complete records or plain lists, so they need no imputer.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"eligible = raw_table.drop_duplicates(\\\"record_id\\\").copy()\\n\", \"eligible = eligible[eligible[\\\"target\\\"].notna() & eligible[\\\"load_pct\\\"].between(0, 100)].copy()\\n\", \"records = records_from_table(eligible)\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 1: Equipment-group split — 10 marks (Week 10)\\n\", \"`group_split(records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Assign whole equipment groups: E1–E4 to train, E5 to validation, E6 to test. Return train, validation and test lists of record_id in input order. Do not use target values to choose membership.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def group_split(records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 2: Baseline, linear and tree predictions — 15 marks (Week 8)\\n\", \"`predict_regression(training_records, feature_records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Fit a training-mean baseline, linear regression and a decision tree (max_depth=4, random_state=17) using load_pct and ambient_c only. Return three prediction lists under baseline, linear and tree. Feature records have no target. Fit preprocessing only on training data. Use SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\") fitted on training records only, separately in each model pipeline. The baseline is the mean training target. Preserve feature order: load_pct, ambient_c.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def predict_regression(training_records, feature_records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 3: MAE and RMSE — 5 marks (Week 8)\\n\", \"`regression_errors(actual, predicted)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Return mae and rmse, calculated over the same paired observations, with full precision.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def regression_errors(actual, predicted):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 4: Classification and cost — 10 marks (Week 9)\\n\", \"`classification_metrics(actual, predicted)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For binary labels return tp, tn, fp, fn, precision, recall and cost=5*fn+fp; use zero for an undefined precision/recall denominator. The inputs are two lists of 0 and 1 labels. Label 1 means a warning was needed (actual) or given (predicted).\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def classification_metrics(actual, predicted):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 5: Group cross-validation — 10 marks (Week 10)\\n\", \"`group_cv_mae(records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The records are clean and have targets. Using whole equipment groups, hold out each of E1, E2, E3, E4 and E5 in turn (E6 is never used). For each fold fit sklearn LinearRegression on load_pct and ambient_c using the other four groups, and compute the MAE on the held-out group. Return folds (the five MAEs in the order E1 to E5), mean (their average) and spread (their sample standard deviation, ddof=1). Do not round. Choose the folds with the equipment_id column and keep the feature order load_pct, ambient_c.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def group_cv_mae(records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 6: Feature ablation — 10 marks (Week 11)\\n\", \"`ablation_metrics(training_records, validation_records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Fit two sklearn LinearRegression models on the same training records: base features [load_pct, ambient_c] and extended features adding load_pct*ambient_c. Evaluate both on the same validation records. Return base_mae, extended_mae and improvement=base_mae-extended_mae, without rounding. Inputs are complete; never fit on validation targets.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def ablation_metrics(training_records, validation_records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 7: Model selection — 10 marks (Week 12)\\n\", \"`select_model(candidates, max_mae, max_ms)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Candidates have name, validation_mae and latency_ms. Keep those meeting both inclusive constraints; select smallest validation_mae, then latency_ms, then name alphabetically. Return the selected name or None. Never use test scores for selection.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def select_model(candidates, max_mae, max_ms):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 8: Ridge and nearest neighbours — 15 marks (Week 13)\\n\", \"`ridge_and_knn(training_records, validation_records)`\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Records are complete and the validation records are held out. Fit on the training records only. Ridge: a pipeline of PolynomialFeatures(3, include_bias=False), StandardScaler() and Ridge(alpha=1.0) on load_pct alone. Nearest neighbours: StandardScaler fitted on the training load_pct and ambient_c, then KNeighborsRegressor(n_neighbors=3). Return ridge_mae and knn_mae, each model's MAE on the validation records without rounding, and better, which is the text \\\"ridge\\\" or \\\"knn\\\" for the smaller MAE. Fit every step on the training records only, and build the ridge curve and the nearest-neighbours model separately.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"For calculations, start with `table = pd.DataFrame(...)`, replacing `...` with the input argument. Calculate from the input passed to your function, not from the global CSV variable. Hidden checks use different numbers with the same columns and rules.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"def ridge_and_knn(training_records, validation_records):\\n\", \"    # Replace this placeholder with your solution.\\n\", \"    raise NotImplementedError(\\\"Complete this task\\\")\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Structured interpretation — 15 marks\\n\", \"Choose exactly one option per question. Each is worth 2.5 marks. These questions use the stated scenarios; do not replace their supplied values with your CSV results.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D1. Why keep all records for an equipment ID in one partition?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: To make all partition sizes equal. | B: To select equipment using target values. | C: To avoid evaluating on equipment already represented during fitting.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D2. Which records may determine the median used to fill missing model inputs?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Training records only; reuse those medians for validation and test. | B: All records before splitting. | C: Test records because they are the newest.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D3. Missed warning costs 5 and a false alarm costs 1. At threshold 0.5 a model makes 4 missed warnings and 2 false alarms. At threshold 0.3 it makes 1 missed warning and 6 false alarms. Which threshold has the lower total cost?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: 0.5, because it has fewer false alarms. | B: 0.3, because its total cost is 11 against 22. | C: Neither; only accuracy can decide.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D4. When may E6 test targets be used?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: While choosing the model with the smallest error. | B: To fit the imputer. | C: Once for final evaluation after model selection is fixed.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D5. A ridge curve gave these validation MAE values in target units: alpha 0.1, 3.4; alpha 1, 2.9; alpha 100, 5.8. Which alpha do you choose?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Alpha 1, because it has the smallest validation MAE. | B: Alpha 0.1, because the smallest penalty fits the training rows best. | C: Alpha 100, because a larger penalty always prevents overfitting.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**D6. A nearest-neighbours model measures distance on load_pct (0 to 100) and ambient_c (about 18 to 35) in their raw units. What should you do before measuring distances?**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A: Nothing; raw units are fine. | B: Scale both inputs, fitting the scaler on the training rows only. | C: Scale both inputs, fitting the scaler on the validation rows as well.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"DECISIONS = {\\\"D1\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D2\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D3\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D4\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D5\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"D6\\\": \\\"\\\"}\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Run your functions on the supplied CSV\\n\", \"Run this supplied cell after implementing the tasks. It calls your functions in the required order and displays their results. This is a check of your work, not an extra task or additional marks. If a function is unfinished, return to that task. Function checks are independent: an unfinished earlier function does not prevent the grader checking later functions on its own inputs.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"partitions = group_split(records)\\n\", \"training = [r for r in records if r[\\\"record_id\\\"] in partitions[\\\"train\\\"]]\\n\", \"validation = [r for r in records if r[\\\"record_id\\\"] in partitions[\\\"validation\\\"]]\\n\", \"features = [{k: v for k, v in r.items() if k != \\\"target\\\"} for r in validation]\\n\", \"predictions = predict_regression(training, features)\\n\", \"actual = [r[\\\"target\\\"] for r in validation]\\n\", \"for name, predicted in predictions.items():\\n\", \"    print(name, regression_errors(actual, predicted))\\n\", \"# E6 is reserved for final evaluation; do not use it to select a model.\\n\", \"complete = [r for r in records if r[\\\"ambient_c\\\"] is not None]\\n\", \"complete_train = [r for r in training if r[\\\"ambient_c\\\"] is not None]\\n\", \"complete_valid = [r for r in validation if r[\\\"ambient_c\\\"] is not None]\\n\", \"print(classification_metrics([0, 1, 1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0, 0, 1]))\\n\", \"print(group_cv_mae(complete))\\n\", \"print(ablation_metrics(complete_train, complete_valid))\\n\", \"print(select_model([dict(name=\\\"linear\\\", validation_mae=1.5, latency_ms=3), dict(name=\\\"tree\\\", validation_mae=1.2, latency_ms=30)], 2.0, 10))\\n\", \"print(ridge_and_knn(complete_train, complete_valid))\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Submit your test\\n\", \"Run the cell below and click **Submit test**. Open the generated form link, check your details and press **Submit** there. Use your own Google account in both Colab and the form. Wait for the form confirmation. If anything fails, contact your lecturer before your personal deadline (or the announced de